# 0099-framework-id-no-rebuild-after-app-update.patch -- the owner's report: after every app update every built game was "Needs setup" (stale), although its build was still valid. Applies
# after 0098. UNTESTED ON DEVICE AND IN THE SIMULATOR: compiled, and its pure part ran in the host harness (below).
#
# Why: the framework stamp (Documents/gameapp.framework, written by the chain) names the app build that made it (app_build=<yyyymmdd-hhmmss>-rust<hash>), and ClassifyFramework compared it with
#   GameAppBuild.Id, which is new at every build of the app: any update made every game stale.
# Now: scripts/build-app.sh computes a FRAMEWORK ID, a hash of only what shapes a built framework and its boot (the host dylib, the stage crates, the tables, the shader chain, the extractor;
#   NOT the C# app, NOT the time, NOT the shader crates: scripts/lib-hash.sh framework_id_listing has the list), and writes it as GameAppBuild.FrameworkId. The app hands it to the chain
#   (gameapp.config `framework_id=`, right after app_build=), the chain stamps it (`framework_id=` after app_build=), and:
#    - GameAppUi.FrameworkMatches (pure; the Rust chain's state::framework_matches is the same rule): the stamp and the app both name a framework id -> FRESH iff they are equal (the build
#      ids do not matter); a stamp from before framework ids -> the build ids decide, as before (so the first update to an app with 0099 rebuilds once, never again for an unchanged host).
#    - ClassifyFramework(onDisk, stamp, build, frameworkId = ""): GatherFacts (Documents) and ParkedReadyGames (the parked slots, 0073) pass GameAppBuild.FrameworkId; the boot-only
#      withdrawal at launch follows GatherFacts().Framework, so a request for a framework of the same framework id is honoured, another id is withdrawn as before.
#    - FrameworkStamp gains FrameworkId (""); "Sign again to play" (0095) keeps the line (StampSignedAgain drops only signature=).
#    - the launch diagnostics line names the framework id ("| framework id <id> |").
#   A different framework id (the host or a stage crate changed) is still a rebuild: "Needs setup", the checklist's "built by an older version of the app", the main button builds.
#   NOT changed on purpose: the shader warm-up's prepared.tsv / warmed.tsv stay keyed on the app build and the chain (the compile runs in the app's own C# code and fills the OS's Metal
#   cache: another app build may compile differently or find the cache gone); the export manifest and the About line keep showing the build id.
#
# VERIFIED (2026-11-22, M1 Pro): check-patches-apply.sh: every patch 0010-0099 applies in order, no fuzz, no offset; the repository's patches give exactly the compiled tree. Compile
#   (-t:Compile, the device project with GameAppBuild.FrameworkId): 0 errors / 40 warnings, the same per file and code as 0098. Host harness (harness99: ... P98;P99): 403 of 403 checks
#   (16 new: the stamp's framework_id, same id after an update -> Fresh / Ready / "Boot again (no rebuild)", another id -> Stale / Needs setup / builds, an older stamp -> build ids, no id
#   on this side -> the old rule, the Rust table, Sign again keeps the line, the wiring of the config, of every ClassifyFramework call, of the withdrawal, the Rust rule, the rewrite cache key
#   and build-app.sh); 7 of 7 mutations killed.
# NOT VERIFIED: nothing ran on a device or in the simulator: an update over a real built game (the first update to 0099 still rebuilds once: the stamps on phones have no framework_id yet).
# NEW STRINGS: the config key "framework_id=" (verify_ipa.sh marker), the diagnostics field "| framework id ". No new UI text.
